1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,120
We've been discussing the factors the architect has to consider when designing domestic buildings.

2
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:13,355
I'm going to move on now to consider the design of public buildings,

3
00:00:13,355 --> 00:00:21,410
and I'll illustrate this by referring to the new Taylor Concert Hall that's recently been completed here in the city.

4
00:00:21,410 --> 00:00:24,400
So, as with a domestic building,

5
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:27,280
when designing a public building

6
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:32,873
an architect needs to consider the function of the building for example,

7
00:00:32,873 --> 00:00:36,200
is it to be used primarily for entertainment

8
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:39,850
or for education, or for administration?

9
00:00:39,850 --> 00:00:46,165
The second thing the architect needs to think about is the context of the building,

10
00:00:46,165 --> 00:00:48,691
this includes its physical location, obviously,

11
00:00:48,691 --> 00:00:56,690
but it also includes the social meaning of the building, how it relates to the people it's built for.

12
00:00:56,690 --> 00:00:59,527
And finally, for an important public building,

13
00:00:59,527 --> 00:01:04,080
the architect may also be looking for a central

14
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:06,011
symbolic idea on which to base the design,

15
00:01:06,011 --> 00:01:12,090
a sort of metaphor for the building and the way in which it is used.

16
00:01:12,090 --> 00:01:17,660
Let's look at the new Taylor Concert Hall in relation to these ideas.

17
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:25,370
The location chosen was a site in a run-down district that has been ignored in previous redevelopment plans.

18
00:01:25,370 --> 00:01:29,760
It was occupied by a factory that had been empty for some years.

19
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:35,934
The whole area was some distance from the high-rise office blocks of the central business district

20
00:01:35,934 --> 00:01:40,950
and shopping centre, but it was only one kilometre from the ring road.

21
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:44,789
The site itself was bordered to the north by a canal

22
00:01:44,789 --> 00:01:48,640
which had once been used by boats bringing in raw materials

23
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:51,070
when the area was used for manufacturing.

24
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:56,220
The architect chosen for the project was Tom Harrison.

25
00:01:56,220 --> 00:02:04,950
He found the main design challenge was the location of the site in an area that had no neighbouring buildings of any importance.

26
00:02:04,950 --> 00:02:12,277
To reflect the fact that the significance of the building in this quite run-down location was as

27
00:02:12,277 --> 00:02:18,742
yet unknown, he decided to create a building centred around the idea of a mystery -

28
00:02:18,742 --> 00:02:22,190
something whose meaning still has to be discovered.

29
00:02:22,190 --> 00:02:27,020
So how was this reflected in the design of the building?

30
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:31,129
Well, Harrison decided to create pedestrian access to the building

31
00:02:31,129 --> 00:02:36,060
and to make use of the presence of water on the site.

32
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:41,520
As people approach the entrance, they therefore have to cross over a bridge.

33
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:48,513
He wanted to give people a feeling of suspense as they see the building first from a distance, and then close-up,

34
00:02:48,513 --> 00:02:55,840
and the initial impression he wanted to create from the shape of the building as a whole was that of a box.

35
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:59,800
The first side that people see, the southern wall

36
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:04,440
is just a high, flat wall uninterrupted by any windows.

37
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:12,390
This might sound off-putting, but it supports Harrison's concept of the building that the person approaching is intrigued

38
00:03:12,390 --> 00:03:15,040
and wonders what will be inside.

39
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:19,775
And this flat wall also has another purpose. At night-time,

40
00:03:19,775 --> 00:03:25,060
projectors are switched on and it functions as a huge screen

41
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:27,350
onto which images are projected.

42
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:35,510
The auditorium itself seats 1500 people.

43
00:03:35,510 --> 00:03:39,340
The floor's supported by ten massive pads.

44
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:41,367
These are constructed from rubber,

45
00:03:41,367 --> 00:03:45,380
and so are able to absorb any vibrations from outside

46
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:48,260
and prevent them from affecting the auditorium.

47
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:52,760
The walls are made of several layers of honey-coloured wood

48
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:55,230
all sourced from local beech trees.

49
00:03:55,230 --> 00:03:59,420
In order to improve the acoustic properties of the auditorium

50
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:01,394
and to amplify the sound,

51
00:04:01,394 --> 00:04:04,270
they are not straight, they are curved.

52
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:09,180
The acoustics are also adjustable according to the size of orchestra

53
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:11,320
and the type of music being played.

54
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:13,386
In order to achieve this,

55
00:04:13,386 --> 00:04:17,540
there are nine movable panels in the ceiling above the orchestra

56
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,998
which are all individually motorized,

57
00:04:19,998 --> 00:04:22,720
and the walls also have curtains

58
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:26,610
which can be opened or closed to change the acoustics.

59
00:04:26,610 --> 00:04:32,270
The reaction of the public to the new building has generally been positive.

60
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:37,800
However, the evaluation of some critics has been less enthusiastic.

61
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:42,210
In spite of Harrison's efforts to use local materials,

62
00:04:42,210 --> 00:04:47,944
they criticise the style of the design as being international rather than local,

63
00:04:47,944 --> 00:04:55,000
and say it doesn't reflect features of the landscape or society for which it is built.
